#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185039} :activity/id #uuid "42cca35e-3169-59f3-9c38-76ccaaa1b972" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050145 :topic/id #uuid "30992ddf-ba31-551a-9df1-d2e25d94df94" :topic/math-academy-id 3820} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184194 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184092 :question/id #uuid "a50a0dd2-10b1-5984-ae1b-6198de3f44ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-7880"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "897ae010-a9a2-5971-a1ec-c2618cb8ad2c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443488}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Population Variance From Sample Data"} :step/id #uuid "d795603a-bac4-5e36-9889-7bf6375caf7b" :step/math-academy-id 29345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185040}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184093 :question/id #uuid "86da2f2b-8028-5807-ba02-63722c4c0f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-7881"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb182688-828c-5252-8d6d-d09c93ba2bc8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443484}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance Using the Alternative Formula"} :step/id #uuid "9f5deae4-70cb-564f-91d8-87a4b24e70ed" :step/math-academy-id 29346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185326 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a sample of size $n,$ the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2.\n$$\n\nWe will now prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ that is,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[S^2\\right] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nA major step towards this goal is to find the expected value of the sum of squares term:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E \\left[ \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 \\right] \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFirst, consider the following sum of squares:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this term by adding and subtracting the sample mean $\\overline X,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left((X_i - \\overline X) + (\\overline X -  \\mu)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSquaring and applying the laws of summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ))}^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}) & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+2(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & = \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}+n(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have used the fact that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i - \\overline X) = 0$ and that $(\\overline X - \\mu)$ does not depend on $i$. Thus, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 =  \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 + n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2 = \\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\mu)^2 -  n(\\overline X-  \\mu)^2  . \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast\\ast)\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is the summation term $(\\ast)$ we wish to find the expectation of.\n\nNow, we make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}] & ={\\sigma }^{2} \\\\ \\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] & =\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinding the expectation of $(\\ast\\ast),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}]-n\\text{E}[(\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu )^{2}] \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-n\\cdot \\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n} \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}-{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}]=\\frac{(n-1){\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the statistic\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\overline X)^2\n$$\n\nis a *biased* estimate of $\\sigma^2.$ Therefore, to form an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we must divide our summation term by $n-1,$ not $n.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{S}^{2}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}\\cdot (n-1){\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "bc43298c-5d5c-5750-8ac9-a9518e92f1bc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6032 :tutorial/title "Proof That the Sample Variance Is an Unbiased Estimator"} :step/id #uuid "2ce2c432-29aa-5494-afaf-43ec20ee1b01" :step/math-academy-id 36799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184112 :question/id #uuid "b3c0e272-0059-59e6-b3ea-55aaecb14cda" :question/math-academy-id "e-9921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443487}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance"} :step/id #uuid "84481413-cf20-5646-8866-e93e72e6dd48" :step/math-academy-id 37321 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184967}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185357 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nWe've already seen that the sample mean $\\overline{X},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator of $\\mu,$ which means that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nThe definition of the sample mean follows naturally from the definition of $\\mu.$\n\nHowever, when constructing estimators for the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ things are not so simple! \n\nFrom the definition of the population variance, you might expect that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nis an *unbiased* estimator for $\\sigma^2.$ However, it can be shown that it is a *biased* estimator of $\\sigma^2,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\\right] \\neq \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nThis might seem surprising, but it's true. \n\nTo compute an *unbiased* estimate of $\\sigma^2,$ we use the so-called **sample variance**. The sample variance is denoted $S^2,$ and is defined as\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we will prove that $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\sigma^2$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Notice that instead of dividing by $n$ (as with our original biased estimator), we instead divide by $n-1.$ This is sometimes referred to as **Bessel's correction**, as it corrects the bias of the original estimator.\n\n- We should think of $S^2$ as a random variable whose value varies according to the particular sample under consideration.\n\n- Since $S^2$ is a random variable, it has a sampling distribution (i.e., a probability distribution). We'll learn more about the sampling distribution of $S^2$ in future lessons.\n\nFinally, we have an alternative formula for the sample variance that's often more convenient in practice:\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right]\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "e496ddcf-d3f6-5835-b2cf-41a60fd869aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6392 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "775ba336-e81a-5214-bcb3-13cce92d553e" :step/math-academy-id 38668 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185040 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185358 :tutorial/content "Here, we will show that\n\n$$\nS^2 =\\dfrac{n}{n-1}\\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right].\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of $S^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses and distributing the summation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}^{2}-2{X}_{i}\\overset{―}{X}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2{X}_{i}\\overset{―}{X}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-2\\overset{―}{X}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider each sum separately:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2 = n\\cdot \\overline{X^2}$ follows directly from the  definition of the mean of $X^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i = n\\cdot \\overline{X}$ follows directly from the  definition of the mean of $X$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\, \\textrm{times}} = n$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}^{2}-2\\overset{―}{X}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}[n\\cdot \\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-2\\overset{―}{X}\\cdot n\\cdot \\overset{―}{X}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}\\cdot n] \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-2{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}+{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "76207c26-f399-5ea4-aec8-b8b994fd8915" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6397 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Alternative Formula"} :step/id #uuid "db6591d9-519a-5e8b-9a10-89dc5a7c761d" :step/math-academy-id 38675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184981}}] :activity/title "The Sample Variance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184405}]}
